  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A plume of tropical moisture characterized by \n2\"+ PWATs and dewpoints in the 70s is forecast to continue to \noverspread the Carolinas to the east of the northward-advancing \nremnants of TC Isaias overnight. At the surface, a warm \nfront/coastal trough will trudge north along the southeastern \ncoastline, likely with a weak, non- tropical area of low \npressure developing along it. Now it appears the main axis of \nconvection will remain over the Gulf Stream, with heavy storms \noffshore robbing some of the rich moisture advection into ENC. \nThis has been a notable trend with most of the CAMs, and the \n10/12Z guidance continues this trend. For this reason, have cont\nto trend down with QPF, especially inland. Most model estimates\nof several inches along the immediate coast, with much lower \namts less than an inch or so for the interior Coastal Plain \ncounties. Will continue Flood Watch for areas along the coast, \neffective through through early Sun afternoon, though bulk of \nwidespread rain will be over by Sun morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Possibility of convective elements reaching the\ncoast would warrant a tornado risk later this evening and esp\novernight as the aforementioned warm front lifts through ENC. \nPlenty of low level helicity in place with the inc serly flow in\nplace and strong veering wind profile downstairs. The greatest \ntor threat would likely be focused near the srn/sern coast where\nsome better instability advects onshore. For Sunday, flow \nbecomes more unidirectional with swrly low level winds ensuing \nduring the afternoon. While helicity wanes, better instability \nwill be in place due to daytime highs expected in the low 80s \nand TD's surging through the mid 70s. Will have to watch for \na severe threat Sunday afternoon ahead of the cold front, with\nbest chances appearing for the interior zones, where NCAR/HRRR\nsvr probs are highest.\n\nThe remnants of Isaias are forecast to round the upper level \nridge and move off the Northern Mid-Atlantic Coast early next \nweek. Drier air then moves in behind it with drying conditions\ndeveloping Sun night into Mon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Strengthening onshore erly winds, building \nseas, and higher than normal astronomical tides may support some\ncoastal impacts through early Mon. There remains uncertainty on\nthe magnitude of impacts, especially given the shorter-duration\nof the stronger winds, as well as what should be a weaker swell\nand periodicity compared to the recent Nor'easter. With that in\nmind, there will be an increased risk of rip currents and large\nwaves, at a minimum. High Surf Advisory remains in effect \nbetween Ocracoke and Oregon Inlets focused overnight into \nSunday. Additionally, some of the most vulnerable locations \nalong the Outer Banks may see at least some minor issues, \nespecially with Sunday morning's high tide (7-9am). There may \nalso be some very minor (sub advisory) coastal flooding around \nsounds and tidal waterways around the Crystal Coast due to the \nhigher astronomical tides.",
